= The Democratic Party
<span class="wide_tooltip-wrap"><span style="color: #E3000F;">* Flavour Content *</span><span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><span>This is a flavour event that does not have a direct impact on gameplay and is extra content purposed for immersion and narration. Though it's advised you keep them for maximum enjoyment of the game, you can turn them off in the Options menu above.</span></span></span>

When multiparty elections took place for the first time and CHP was decrowned for the first time in 1950, two men were the architects of this victory: Adnan Menderes and Celal Bayar. The first was the public face of the Democrat Party who rallied the rural masses and the newly fostered Turkish bourgeoisie against the Kemalist CHP with his agrarian and right wing populism. The latter used to represent the liberal wing of CHP in the 1930's, even serving as Atatürk's and İnönü's prime minister between 1937 and 1939.

When the coup of 1960 happened and Adnan Menderes was hang for his persecution of the legal opposition and increased authoritarianization, Celal Bayar was spared for his old age, about to turn 80 at that time. As he was banned from politics for his presidency during Menderes's premiership, many right wing parties rose who did not truly represent the populist and agrarian vision of DP. As the technocrats under Süleyman Demirel took over AP, the right was left without a true determined force. However it would all change when DP was formed in 1970.

DP, or the **Democratic Party** was founded in 1970 by the right wing of AP who broke off from AP and is led by Ferruh Bozbeyli, a young attorney who defended the persecuted Democrat Party politicians after the 1960 coup. Yet it should be seen that DP's real electoral strength comes from the support of Celal Bayar who is getting nearing 90 and time is slipping from within his hands. DP's electoral success depends on its ability to establish itself as an independent political force apart from AP; else, the anti military winds currently sailing the party forward may cease with DP left in the middle of the sea as a relic of Turkish electoral history without reaching any of its goals.
  
DP is a relic of the past indeed, and is seeking to reclaim the title of 'the party of the right' as the once powerful Democrat Party did. Even though we have been ideological opponents in the last 25 years, perhaps our mutual desire to weaken AP may lead to cooperation between the two parties.


- @root: Demirkırat against time.
